  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tomato processing mapping</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Algeria has a large horticulture sector comprising of over half a million hectares of vegetable production. With a growing population, Algeria&#x2019;s strategy is to reduce reliance on imports. But Algeria is considered a water scarce country. At present, 59% of the available water comes from ground water and 24% from surface water, of which around 65% goes to agricultural use.Besides an increasing demand for food, development and modernisation of the agro-food sector, the sector is also pursued by the government as a means of economic diversification and attracting investment outside the energy sector.Since several years, Algeria developed profitable local tomato processing industry to cater the domestic demand of tomato concentrate. The tomato processing sector, however, needs modernisation throughout the value chain to optimise the use of precious resources such as water, reduce produce losses and optimise earnings.  Algerian horticulture sector can provide interesting opportunities for Dutch suppliers because of its size, the current technology level and existing development needs. This mapping of the Algerian tomato processing value chain and landscape serves to gain the necessary insights of the greatest opportunities for development and investment.</narrative>
  </description>
